## Security Analysis: IDOR Vulnerability Assessment

### Root Cause of IDOR in Invoice/Order Endpoints

IDOR vulnerabilities occur when endpoints accept a resource identifier (e.g., `/invoices/42`) and fetch the record directly by ID without verifying the requesting user's organization owns that record. An authenticated user from Org A can enumerate and access Org B's invoices/orders by simply incrementing integers.

### Attack Vectors Identified

| Vector | Example | Risk |
|--------|---------|------|
| Sequential ID guessing | `GET /invoices/1001` → belongs to another tenant | HIGH |
| Cross-tenant write | `PUT /orders/500` mutates another org's order | CRITICAL |
| Bulk enumeration via list endpoint missing org filter | `GET /invoices?page=2` returns all orgs' data | HIGH |
| Soft-delete bypass | `DELETE /invoices/300` on foreign record | HIGH |
| Status transition abuse | `POST /orders/200/confirm` on foreign order | HIGH |

### Architectural Defense Strategy

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Request Layer                                           │
│  JWT → extract org_id claim (verified, not trusted body) │
└──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┘
