## Requirements Analysis

### Core Goals
1. **SQL Injection Protection** — Sanitize and validate dynamic query filter inputs (field names, operators, values) used in parameterized query builders.
2. **XSS Protection** — Sanitize user profile fields (display name, bio, website URL, avatar URL) before persistence and before rendering.

### Architecture Decomposition

```
validation-pipeline/
├── src/
│   ├── validators/
│   │   ├── base.validator.ts          # Abstract base validator interface
│   │   ├── sql-filter.validator.ts    # SQL query filter validation
│   │   └── xss-profile.validator.ts  # XSS profile field sanitization
│   ├── sanitizers/
│   │   ├── sql.sanitizer.ts           # SQL injection sanitizer
│   │   └── xss.sanitizer.ts           # XSS sanitizer (HTML escape + URL validation)
│   ├── pipeline/
│   │   ├── validation-pipeline.ts     # Pipeline orchestrator
│   │   └── pipeline-result.ts         # Typed result/error envelope
│   ├── types/
│   │   ├── sql-filter.types.ts        # Query filter domain types
│   │   └── profile.types.ts           # User profile domain types
│   └── index.ts                       # Public API barrel
├── tests/
│   ├── sql-filter.validator.test.ts
│   ├── xss-profile.validator.test.ts
│   ├── sql.sanitizer.test.ts
│   ├── xss.sanitizer.test.ts
│   └── pipeline.integration.test.ts
├── package.json
├── tsconfig.json
└── jest.config.js
```

### Security Threat Model

| Threat | Vector | Mitigation |
|--------|--------|------------|
| SQL Injection (classic) | `' OR 1=1 --` in filter values | Parameterized query enforcement; value escaping |
| SQL Injection (column) | `users; DROP TABLE` in field names | Strict allowlist of permitted field names |
| SQL Injection (operator) | `=1; DROP TABLE` | Strict operator enum |
| Stored XSS | `<script>` in bio/display name | HTML entity encoding |
| DOM-based XSS | `javascript:` in URL fields | URL protocol allowlist |
| Attribute injection | `" onmouseover=` in names | Attribute-context escaping |
| Null byte injection | `\x00` in any field | Null byte stripping |
| Unicode homoglyph | Lookalike chars in names | Normalization to NFC |

### Edge Cases
- Empty strings vs. null vs. undefined — all treated distinctly
- Maximum field length enforcement
- Multi-byte Unicode in display names (emoji, RTL chars)
- Nested filter conditions (AND/OR compound queries)
- Numeric vs. string values in SQL filters
- URL fields: relative vs. absolute, http vs. https

