## Requirements Analysis

### Core Features
1. **Organization-scoped API keys** — Keys are owned by an organization, optionally tied to a specific user/service account
2. **BCrypt hashed storage** — Raw key shown once at creation; only hash stored at rest
3. **Permission scoping** — Granular `read`, `write`, `admin` permissions as a bitmask + structured scope set
4. **Last-used timestamp telemetry** — Non-blocking async update of `last_used_at` on every authenticated request

### Edge Cases & Security Considerations

| Concern | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Timing attacks on key comparison | `bcrypt.compare()` constant-time compare (never raw string equality) |
| Key enumeration | Keys returned without hash; only prefix stored for display |
| Concurrent last-used writes | Upsert with `IF last_used_at < now()` to avoid write amplification |
| Key rotation race | Atomic `deactivate-then-create` in a DB transaction |
| Privilege escalation | `admin` scope required to create/revoke keys; enforced at middleware level |
| Brute-force | Rate limiting middleware (token bucket per org) |
| Leaked keys | `key_prefix` (first 8 chars) stored plaintext for audit; full key never logged |
| bcrypt cost factor | `12` — balances security and auth latency (~300ms) |

